ope expects a JSON with assignment values for a..h only. There's no other output field.\n\nThus we just need to output a single assignment. The \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component\" might be a generic instruction for other tasks; but here we just need to fill assignment.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP. Let's list constraints clearly.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: allowed pairs for (a,b): [ [0,0], [1,3], [0,1], [2,1], [3,1] ]\n\nc2: (b,c): allowed pairs: [ [0,0], [3,1], [2,2], [1,3], [1,2] ]\n\nc3: (c,d): allowed: [ [0,3], [3,3], [2,0], [2,1], [3,0] ]\n\nc4: (d,a): allowed: [ [3,0], [0,0], [2,3], [1,2], [3,1] ]\n\nc5: (e,f): allowed: [ [1,3], [2,3], [3,1], [0,1], [3,3] ]\n\nc6: (f,g): allowed: [ [3,3], [0,2], [2,0], [3,2], [3,0] ]\n\nc7: (g,h): allowed: [ [3,3], [1,0], [3,0], [2,2], [0,3] ]\n\nc8: (h,e): allowed: [ [3,1], [0,2], [0,3], [1,1], [1,0] ]\n\nc9: (b,e): allowed: [ [0,1], [3,0] ]\n\nc10: (d,g): allowed: [ [3,3], [0,3] ]\n\nGoal: assign a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h ∈ {0,1,2,3} satisfying all.\n\nWe can brute force manually? There are 4^8 = 65536 possibilities, but we can reason.\n\nLet's treat constraints stepwise.\n\nFirst, c9: (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,1) or (3,0). So either b=0 and e=1, or b=3 and e=0.\n\nc10: (d,g) allowed pairs: (3,3) or (0,3). So either d=3,g=3 or d=0,g=3.\n\nThus g is always 3? Both options have g=3. Indeed both allowed pairs have second component 3. So g=3 is forced. Then d can be either 3 or 0.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) allowed pairs: g=3, h must be such that (3,h) is allowed. Allowed pairs for (g,h): [ [3,3], [1,0], [3,0], [2,2], [0,3] ]. So allowed (3,3) and (3,0). So h can be 3 or 0.\n\nThus h ∈ {3,0}.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed pairs: depends on h. Allowed pairs: [ [3,1], [0,2], [0,3], [1,1], [1,0] ].\n\nIf h=3: then (3,e) must be allowed: only [3,1] fits (3,1). So e must be 1.\n\nIf h=0: then (0,e) allowed: [0,2] and [0,3] both have e=2 or 3. So e ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus possibilities:\n\nCase 1: h=3 => e=1.\n\nCase 2: h=0 => e ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) allowed pairs: allowed pairs for (e,f): [ [1,3], [2,3], [3,1], [0,1], [3,3] ]. So for each e, f must be one of allowed.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: g=3, so (f,3) must be allowed. Allowed pairs for (f,g): [ [3,3], [0,2], [2,0], [3,2], [3,0] ]. So allowed (f,3) only if there is a pair with second component 3. Looking at list: [3,3] is (f=3,g=3). Others have second component 2,0,0,2. So only (f=3,g=3) works. So f must be 3.\n\nThus f=3 forced.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) = (e,3) must be allowed. Allowed pairs: check each: [1,3] => e=1 works. [2,3] => e=2 works. [3,1] => e=3 but f=1 not 3, so not. [0,1] => f=1 not 3. [3,3] => e=3 works. So allowed e values for f=3 are {1,2,3}. So e ∈ {1,2,3}.\n\nCombine with earlier constraints on e from h.\n\nCase 1: h=3 => e=1 (fits e ∈ {1,2,3}). Good.\n\nCase 2: h=0 => e ∈ {2,3} (fits). So both possible.\n\nThus we have two main branches.\n\nNow c9 gave relation b and e: either (b=0,e=1) or (b=3,e=0). But e is either 1,2,3. So only (b=0,e=1) matches e=1 case. The other branch e=0 is impossible because e cannot be 0 (domain includes 0 but not allowed by e constraints). So b must be 0 and e=1. That forces h=3 (since e=1 only occurs when h=3). So we have determined:\n\n- e=1\n- b=0\n- h=3\n\nCheck: c9: (b,e) = (0,1) allowed. Good.\n\nNow we have f=3, g=3.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) = (1,3) allowed. Yes.\n\nc6: (f,g) = (3,3) allowed. Yes.\n\nc7: (g,h) = (3,3) allowed. Yes.\n\nc8: (h,e) = (3,1) allowed. Yes.\n\nThus the right side variables are set: e=1, f=3, g=3, h=3.\n\nNow we need to assign a,b,c,d satisfying left side constraints c1..c4 plus c10 (d,g). We have b=0, g=3, d unknown (0 or 3 per c10). So c10: (d,g) = (d,3) allowed only if (d=3,g=3) because only pair (3,3) allowed. So d must be 3. So d=3.\n\nThus d=3.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) = (c,3) allowed pairs: c3 allowed: [ [0,3], [3,3], [2,0], [2,1], [3,0] ]. So allowed (c,3) only if second component is 3: we have (0,3) and (3,3). So c can be 0 or 3.\n\nc4: (d,a) = (3,a) allowed pairs: c4 allowed: [ [3,0],